 Museum of Art, Cleveland, OH (organizer) (February 12-April 20, 1986).", "opening_date": "1986-02-12T05:00:00"}, {"id": 310026, "title": "Byobu: The Art of the Japanese Screen", "description": "<i>Byobu: The Art of the Japanese Screen</i>. The Cleveland Museum of Art, Cleveland, OH (organizer) (December 8, 1987-January 10, 1988).", "opening_date": "1987-12-08T05:00:00"}], "legacy": [{"description": "<em>Main Asian Rotation (Gallery 236)</em>. The Cleveland Museum of Art, Cleveland, OH (January 13-November 16, 2015).", "opening_date": "2015-01-13T00:00:00"}]}, "provenance": [{"description": "(Klaus F. Naumann East Asian Art, Tokyo, Japan, sold to the Cleveland Museum of Art)", "citations": [], "footnotes": null, "date": "?–1985", "sortorder": 1}, {"description": "The Cleveland Museum of Art, Cleveland, OH", "citations": [], "footnotes": null, "date": "1985–", "sortorder": 2}], "find_spot": null, "related_works": [], "former_accession_numbers": ["1985.135"], "did_you_know": null, "early_education_description": null, "artlens_description": null, "description": "A tiger looks upon a waterfall as it prowls out of a bamboo grove on this screen, and a dragon dives through clouds on the right. Tiger and dragon are traditional symbols of the balancing forces in the world, yin (the feminine aspect) and yang (the masculine aspect). This painting has a signature and seals, identifying it as a work by Soga Nichokuan, a painter thought to be from Sakai (near Osaka), and known primarily for his representations of hawk-eagles (<em>kumataka</em>).", "external_resources": {"wikidata": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q60475386"], "internet_archive": ["https://archive.org/details/clevelandart-1985.134.2-tiger"]}, "citations": [{"citation": "Turner, Evan H. “The Year in Review for 1985.” <em>The Bulletin of the Cleveland Museum of Art</em> 73, no. 2 (February 1986): 26–71.", "page_number": "Reproduced: p. 46; Mentioned: p. 70, no. 188", "url": "http://www.jstor.org/stable/25159930"}, {